---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/data';
---
<Layout title="Getting started" description="A starting point for your workshop, character and equipment.">
 <p class="eyebrow">Handbook / Basics</p><h1>Getting started</h1><p class="intro">Set up your first workshop, learn your weapon, identify equipment and plan your character.</p>
 <p><a class="button button-primary" href={href('guides/tutorial/')}>Open the in-game tutorial</a></p>
 <nav class="toc-inline" aria-label="Getting started sections"><a id="workshop-builds" href={href('tutorials/')}>Build guides</a><a href="#first-steps">First evening</a><a href="#combat">Combat</a><a href="#factory">Your workshop</a><a href="#appraisal">Dungeon gear</a><a href="#skills">Your build</a><a href={href('guides/player-shops/')}>Player shops</a></nav>
 <div class="prose">
 <section id="first-steps"><p class="eyebrow">01 / A place to begin</p><h2>Your first evening</h2><p>Choose somewhere you want to return to. Secure a bed, storage and a steady meal before turning a promising clearing into a permanent workshop. Leave room around your machinery: Create production is easier to understand when you can see each step.</p><ol><li><strong>Establish your basics.</strong> Gather tools and food, mark your home and explore the immediate area.</li><li><strong>Choose a small production goal.</strong> Find its recipe and work backward through the components rather than building every machine at once.</li><li><strong>Connect the first mechanism.</strong> Use the opening <a href={href('chapters/')}>progression chapters</a> to understand the manufacturing path.</li><li><strong>Take a short expedition.</strong> Bring a weapon you understand, room for finds and a reliable way home.</li></ol></section>
 <section id="combat"><p class="eyebrow section">02 / Preparation</p><h2>Combat basics</h2><p>Epic Fight gives a weapon a sequence of attacks, a grip and an innate skill. Those behaviors are part of its identity. A short dagger, a thrusting rapier and a heavy greatsword ask for different spacing and timing.</p><ol><li>Find your weapon in the <a href={href('weapons/')}>weapon registry</a>. Check the assigned style and grip.</li><li>Practice the regular combo, running attack and aerial attack somewhere safe.</li><li>Watch the innate skill and learn when it becomes available. Dual-wield styles can change their attacks when a compatible weapon occupies the other hand.</li><li>Compare your equipment to the encounter and your character build. A material upgrade does not automatically change a standard sword into a signature weapon.</li></ol><p>Magic casting staffs remain casting weapons. Physical battlestaffs and massive slam weapons are a separate family.</p></section>
 <section id="factory"><p class="eyebrow section">03 / Production</p><h2>Plan a production line</h2><p>Choose an output you will use repeatedly. Find it in the <a href={href('recipes/')}>recipe registry</a>, inspect its ingredients and arrange the process so the next step can take what the previous one produces.</p><ol><li><strong>Read the recipe’s process.</strong> Crafting, pressing, mixing, deploying and sequenced assembly require different machinery.</li><li><strong>Check the real gate.</strong> The mechanism or machine ingredient tells you which part of progression the recipe belongs to.</li><li><strong>Keep incomplete items moving.</strong> A sequenced assembly uses a work-in-progress item across its stages. Feed it through the listed operations and loop count.</li><li><strong>Separate outputs from byproducts.</strong> Give the useful output a clear destination and leave space for extra products.</li><li><strong>Look up the setup.</strong> Use the <a href={href('tutorials/')}>build guides</a> for diagrams and instructions.</li></ol></section>
 <section id="appraisal"><p class="eyebrow section">04 / Rewards</p><h2>Turn a dungeon find into useful equipment.</h2><p>An unidentified item is a possibility rather than a finished decision. Appraisal reveals the equipment so you can compare it to what your character actually needs.</p><ol><li>Bring unidentified rewards back from your expedition.</li><li>Use the identification route and pay the required experience or machine resources.</li><li>Compare the resulting weapon or armor, its rarity and affixes against your current build.</li><li>Equip the useful pieces and use the salvage loop for the rest.</li></ol><p>The <a href={href('loot/')}>equipment and appraisal reference</a> explains the station, rarity and resource rules.</p></section>
 <section id="skills"><p class="eyebrow section">05 / Character</p><h2>Plan a character build</h2><p>The character tree is a connected set of professions, not a requirement to choose one permanent class. Begin at a root, follow neighboring nodes and invest across branches where they support your play.</p><ol><li>Open the <a href={href('skills/')}>skilltree planner</a> and inspect the six starting branches.</li><li>Set a point budget and select the skills that matter most to your build.</li><li>Watch the spending requirements on advanced nodes. A connected route still needs enough points already invested.</li><li>Review flat bonuses, base multipliers and conditional passives separately.</li><li>Use the share link to keep or discuss a build.</li></ol><p>Your <a href={href('mechanics/#origins')}>Origin</a>, equipment and <a href={href('mechanics/#titles')}>titles</a> work alongside these choices.</p></section>
 </div>
</Layout>

<script>
  const movedGuides: Record<string, string> = {
    '#walking-machines': 'guides/walking-machines/',
    '#workshop-builds': 'tutorials/#workshop-builds',
  };

  function followMovedGuide() {
    const destination = movedGuides[location.hash];
    if (destination) location.replace(import.meta.env.BASE_URL + destination);
  }

  window.addEventListener('hashchange', followMovedGuide);
  followMovedGuide();
</script>
